{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"agent_message","text":"{\"A\":\"Explore a sparse-obstacle combinatorial path-counting branch, with a dense rolling-row fallback. Base the branch decision on estimated work, including obstacle-pair cost and preprocessing, rather than a fixed threshold tied to observed inputs. Preserve correct handling of duplicate obstacles, blocked start/destination, and all valid N and M. The two measured A candidates (0.04899s and 0.04972s) improved on baseline but trail the best B result; focus on reducing setup and pairwise overhead, and retain this direction only if the full correctness and timing gates support it.\",\"B\":\"Use the fastest measured B candidate as the research baseline: sorted (anti-diagonal,row) obstacles, two O(N) rolling buffers, independent unmasked diagonal loops, and sparse zeroing after each diagonal (0.00840s mean; the timestamp-array variant measured 0.00885s). Tune the diagonal kernel and data handling for the fixed ARM64, four-core target, investigating vectorization or parallel work only where synchronization costs are justified. Preserve the O(N+M) memory footprint and validate boundaries, including N=1 and blocked endpoints; compare complete-program measurements, not kernel-only estimates.\",\"C\":\"Investigate whether the tiled anti-diagonal wavefront can be improved through tile-size and serial/parallel crossover tuning for the fixed four-core target. The 32×32 tiled candidate measured 0.01275s, slower than B but faster than baseline; the row-chunk summary approach measured 0.03674s and should not be pursued without new evidence. Keep a low-overhead serial path, preserve sparse obstacle storage and correctness for every valid input, and retain parallel complexity only if end-to-end measurements show a repeatable benefit.\",\"G\":\"Treat the fastest measured admissible candidate, B's 0.00840s result, as the provisional integration anchor; revalidate it before relying on it. Integrate any A or C contribution only as an isolated, reviewable change, then measure the complete integrated program and run the unchanged correctness gate. Do not infer additive gains from standalone timings: keep the measured best complete version if a combination regresses, and preserve correctness for all valid inputs without changing tests, oracle, compiler, budgets, target, or permissions.\",\"analysis\":\"The supplied source is Problem D's grid-path dynamic program, so the objectives target that computation rather than the other problems in the statement. The baseline uses an N×N blocked bitmap and rolling rows; its measured mean is 0.07955s and its correctness result covers four cases. Among the listed candidate means, B's sparse anti-diagonal implementation is fastest (0.00840s), followed by B's timestamp variant (0.00885s), C's tiled wavefront (0.01275s), C's chunk-summary approach (0.03674s), and the two A candidates (0.04899s and 0.04972s). These are run-specific observations, not guarantees about other valid inputs or about integrated combinations; G should make its choice from complete-program correctness and timing measurements.\"}"}}
